{
  "dom_id": "step-q235974",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14163281/assets/d24c4077069b1de16c64e3179b8e60094e0440ed0a95ac3525297af85899bec2.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m∠A=m∠B.$\n\n$m∠C=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
  "worked_solution": "Using $m∠A=m∠B,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠B \\\\ 8w-{2}^{∘} & =9w-{10}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={8}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $w={8}^{∘}$ in the expression for $m∠A,$ we get\n\n$$\nm∠A=8({8}^{∘})-{2}^{∘}={62}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m∠C$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠A+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {62}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {124}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
